From e59cf75f7fec3afd3095778f72234be5b1917b82 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 01:12:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GBM] A 3D LUT for SDR and one for HDR for each
 display: settings, kodi.log and the player information

New group "3D LUT (display calibration)" in System > Display, after the
mapping to the display, expert level, off by default:
videoscreen.hadeslut3d (on/off), videoscreen.hadeslut3dsdr and
videoscreen.hadeslut3dhdr (a .cube, .3dl or .3dlut file, chosen with Kodi's
file browser), videoscreen.hadeslut3drange (full range or video levels, for
a .cube or .3dl, which do not say) and videoscreen.hadeslut3dsdrgamut (the
gamut an SDR LUT was made for: BT.709, BT.2020 or DCI-P3). Kodi's own
colour management settings (videoscreen.cmsenabled and the rest) are of its
desktop OpenGL renderer, one madVR LUT for every video and display: not
reused.

A calibration is of one display: the LUTs are kept for each display, by the
make and model of its EDID, in special://profile/hades-display-luts.txt
(DisplayLut.h, CWinSystemGbm). A LUT chosen in the settings is noted for
the display in use; the settings show the LUTs of the display that Kodi
comes to (set on the main thread, as the mode of the menus of each display
is chosen); a display not seen gets none, so a projector's LUT is never
applied to the TV.

What a picture goes out as decides which LUT it takes (DisplayLut.h
TakeOf(), CWinSystemGbmGLESContext::LutFor()):
- SDR: HDR10, HDR10+, HLG and Dolby Vision mapped to SDR, and SDR videos
  that the bridge draws with an upscaler;
- HDR: HDR10 and HDR10+ adapted to the display, Dolby Vision as HDR10
  mapped to it, and Dolby Vision Low-Latency;
- none: native Dolby Vision that the TV maps (its tunnel is bit-exact), HDR
  that the display maps itself (passthrough, not mapped, over the tunnel:
  the display's tone mapping comes after the LUT and changes with each
  film's metadata, so no LUT of that display holds there; madVR offers it
  for want of another way, and here the mapping to the display is that
  way), and the pictures of Kodi's own renderer.
A file that says what it was made for (madVR's transfer and primaries) is
refused for another kind of picture; an SDR LUT is refused where the SDR
picture goes out in another gamut than the one it was made for.

The player information says it on its Video page, in one line under the
zoom to the active area (hades.video.lut): the name of the file where it is
applied (cut in the middle beyond 32 characters), or why it is not. kodi.log
says each decision once when it changes (the file read, its format, size,
range and what it says it is made for; applied to what, or not and why),
and a notice on the screen says, once for each file and reason, that a
chosen LUT cannot be applied for a fault: a file that cannot be read, a LUT
made for another kind of picture, a GPU that could not draw it.

Strings: core #62800 to #62819 (en_gb), Estuary #54800 to #54819 (en_gb,
es_es, es_ar, es_mx).
